---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Development Roadmap | ArcadeDuck"
  description="ArcadeDuck development roadmap, release milestones, supported hardware expansion, stabilization periods, and future PlayStation-derived arcade systems."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Development Roadmap</p>

    <h1>Where ArcadeDuck goes next</h1>

    <p class="page-description">
      ArcadeDuck development is milestone-driven rather than tied to a rigid
      release calendar. New hardware families are implemented in stages, with
      dedicated time between major milestones for bug reports, fixes, testing,
      and community feedback.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current Milestone</p>
        <h2>September 19, 2026 — First official release</h2>
      </div>


    </div>

    <div class="systems-list">
      <article class="hardware-card supported-hardware">
        <div class="hardware-card-header">
          <div>
            <span class="hardware-manufacturer">ArcadeDuck 0.5.x</span>
            <h3>Initial public release</h3>
          </div>

          <span class="status-badge supported">9/19/2026</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The first official ArcadeDuck release establishes the initial
          supported hardware baseline and moves the project from private
          development into public testing.
        </p>

        <div class="hardware-tags">
          <span>Konami GV</span>
          <span>Konami GQ</span>
          <span>Namco System 11</span>
          <span>Sony ZN-1 Families</span>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Release Cadence</p>
        <h2>Build. Stabilize. Repeat.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>
        Each major hardware implementation is followed by a stabilization
        period before development moves on to the next platform.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Step 1</span>
        <h3>Implement hardware</h3>

        <p>
          Bring the next arcade platform into ArcadeDuck with enough hardware,
          controls, audio, media, protection, and peripheral support to make
          meaningful testing possible.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Step 2</span>
        <h3>Release and test</h3>

        <p>
          Put the implementation in front of real users, real cabinets, and
          whatever hardware combinations they manage to throw at it.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Step 3</span>
        <h3>Stabilize</h3>

        <p>
          Spend roughly two weeks working through bug reports, regressions,
          control issues, compatibility problems, and useful suggestions.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Step 4</span>
        <h3>Move forward</h3>

        <p>
          Once the current milestone is stable enough, development shifts to
          the next PlayStation-derived arcade hardware family.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="systems-status-section">
    <div class="systems-group-heading">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Tentative Roadmap</p>
        <h2>Major hardware milestones</h2>
      </div>

      <p>
        Everything after the initial release is intentionally flexible.
        Hardware research, reverse engineering, regressions, and community
        feedback can all change the order or timing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="planned-system-grid">
      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Milestone 01</span>
        <h3>Initial Release</h3>

        <p>
          Konami GV, Konami GQ, Namco System 11, and all currently implemented
          Sony ZN-1-based arcade hardware.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Recovery Period</span>
        <h3>Two weeks away from major implementation work</h3>

        <p>
          A short break after the initial release so my brain can cool off
          after staring at emulator code for six-plus hours a day and
          pretending that qualifies as a normal hobby.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Stabilization</span>
        <h3>Initial release fixes</h3>

        <p>
          Roughly two weeks dedicated to bug reports, compatibility problems,
          control issues, suggestions, and whatever entirely new ways people
          discover to break ArcadeDuck.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Milestone 02</span>
        <h3>Capcom ZN-2</h3>

        <p>
          Implement the second-generation Capcom ZN hardware family, followed
          by another stabilization and community-testing period.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Milestone 03</span>
        <h3>Taito G-Net</h3>

        <p>
          Bring Taito G-Net into the main ArcadeDuck hardware lineup, including
          its removable media, protection, and platform-specific behavior,
          followed by another bug-fix cycle.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Milestone 04</span>
        <h3>Namco System 12</h3>

        <p>
          Continue the Namco hardware path with System 12, followed by another
          dedicated stabilization period before moving to the next platform.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="planned-system-card">
        <span class="hardware-manufacturer">Future Milestones</span>
        <h3>System 10, System 573, Twinkle, and beyond</h3>

        <p>
          Continue working through the remaining PlayStation-derived arcade
          hardware families as research, implementation quality, and available
          brain cells permit.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>


  <section class="systems-note">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Roadmap Policy</p>
      <h2>The order matters more than the dates.</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      September 19 is the first public release target. Everything after that is
      intentionally tentative. Arcade hardware has a habit of turning one
      apparently simple subsystem into three weeks of reverse engineering, so
      ArcadeDuck will move forward when the current milestone is stable enough
      rather than chasing arbitrary deadlines.
    </p>
  </section>


  <section class="systems-note">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">The Long-Term Plan</p>
      <h2>Keep going until there is nothing left to implement.</h2>
    </div>

    <p>
      Implement hardware, release it, fix what breaks, then move on to the next
      platform. Repeat until I either run out of PlayStation-based arcade
      hardware or remaining brain cells.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>